).</p><p><strong>Ladd McConkey</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 LV surrender 0.44 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><p>In the red zone LV throw it (59% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Tre Tucker</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p><strong>Indoors</strong> \u2014 a dome removes weather variance and historically nudges passing efficiency up; a small tailwind for the passing games.</p><h3>LAC \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>LAC are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>40%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>This is the profile that most rewards a lead back. As a 8-point favorite projected to lead for 39% of snaps, <strong>LAC</strong> will be running to close the game, and their run-first identity compounds it. <strong>Omarion Hampton</strong> is the beneficiary \u2014 we project <strong>10 carries for 44 yards</strong>, and his ceiling is where the value sits: in the simulations where this becomes a two-score game, his volume spikes as LAC salt it away.</p><p>The flip side: <strong>Ladd McConkey</strong> (4 targets, 40 yards) has his ceiling <em>capped</em> by the same script that helps the run game \u2014 when LAC lead and run, there simply aren't as many pass attempts to go around. He's a safer floor than ceiling play in this spot.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Oronde Gadsden II</strong> (TE, 3 targets, 30 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>LV \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>LV are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (46% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 LV project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Ashton Jeanty</strong>'s rushing projection (16 carries, 61 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Tre Tucker</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, LV funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>6 targets for 44 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Brock Bowers</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 45 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 8-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"LV","col":"#C9D2DC","players":[{"name":"Kirk Cousins","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":227.75,"book":null,"over_th":214.88,"under_th":239.12,"call":null,"edge":null},{"label":"Pass TDs","proj":1.53,"book":null,"over_th":1.02,"under_th":1.4,"call":null,"edge":null}],"td":{"p